  "(23:00-27:30) Resolution machinery: MAX_REVIEW_RETRY_ATTEMPTS=3 cap ('if they can't, they're going to let us know and we hop back in the loop to fix our agentic layer... for the class of issue'); resolve_review_issues loops blockers only; create_and_implement_patch → /patch writes a focused patch plan (like chore/bug/feature, a template meta prompt) → /implement (higher-order prompt, reused) executes it.",
  "(27:30-31:40) Check progress in agents/{adw_id}/ (build phase → review phase); branch changed under him — 'our agent is in full control over this system'; git diff --stat origin/main shows plan, e2e test generated from the plan template, implementation. R2 upload enabled: public bucket ready.",
  "(31:41-33:30) Workflow completes: issue comment shows uploaded screenshots. The proof reveals the bug — export arrow present but NOT left of the hide button ('our agent did not obey this properly'). Review summary says all tests passed, one-click export implemented. 'There is work to be done on improving the prompt and the workflow for this review process.'",
  "(33:30-35:20) /start the app, click through: downloads work (users table CSV, query-result CSV), but placement is wrong. Post-mortem: the test step was SKIPPED (plan_build_review, not plan_build_test_review) — 'the UI test very likely would have caught this'; and the review agent never reads its own screenshots: .mcp.json has no --vision, 'the tokens aren't flowing through the vision capabilities.' Fix belongs in the prompt ('for every image you take, be sure to read it in') not the global MCP config.",
  "(35:20-37:45) Patch it like you're out-loop: comment on issue 14 containing 'adw_patch' + 'make sure the download button is just to the left of our hide button'; uv run adws/adw_patch.py 14 <adw-id> (after first passing 15 — the PR number — by mistake); patch plan lands in specs/patch/patch-adw-490eb6b5-fix-download-button-position.md; implement runs; refresh → 'Export is now right next to hide.' 'Why didn't you just do that by hand? You're missing the point... build the system that builds the system.'",
  "(42:30-45:30) Document the feature on the same state: uv run adws/adw_document.py 14 <same-adw-id>. It sees the git diff + spec, writes app_docs/feature-490eb6b5-one-click-table-exports.md (concise breakdown, files changed, how to use, screenshots embedded — note the screenshot still shows the pre-patch layout because review wasn't rerun: another named improvement), copying images to app_docs/assets/.",
  "(45:30-48:00) The magical part: /document also UPDATES .claude/commands/conditional_docs.md with the new doc + conditions ('When working with CSV export functionality'). Search 'conditional docs' → referenced inside bug, chore, feature, patch, and prime. Plan reads what document wrote: 'This is a full, complete loop... This seals the deal. This connects them all.'",
  "(48:00-53:30) Zoom out on the issue/PR: a couple of prompts → ~900 lines of engineering work — plan, implementation, generated e2e test, patch, docs, conditional-docs update, PR. Agents present the work with proof; review velocity is the point; encode YOUR verification (report/image/video — a live asset) whatever your domain (scikit models, analytics, prototypes).",
  "(53:30-57:16) KPI honesty pass: attempts 1→2 (patch), size solid, streak reset, presence ~3 vs the ideal 2 (prompt input + review/merge). Teasers: lesson 7 reveals the secret of Tactical Agentic Coding ('hiding in plain sight'); dangerous-mode ADWs shipping to production for small bugs/chores — 'do not wait to start putting this stuff in your codebase.'"
]
[
  {
    "text": "We nearly arrive at the future where you can run a single prompt that triggers a fleet of agents that ship your work end to end.",
    "approx_timestamp": "0:28"
  },
  {
    "text": "Testing answers the question, does it work? But review answers the question, is what we built what we asked for?",
    "approx_timestamp": "2:24"
  },
  {
    "text": "We want attempts down, streak up, size up, presence down.",
    "approx_timestamp": "3:18"
  },
  {
    "text": "Documentation provides feedback on work done for future agents to reference in their work.",
    "approx_timestamp": "3:52"
  },
  {
    "text": "The tactic is one agent, one prompt, one purpose.",
    "approx_timestamp": "4:33"
  },
  {
    "text": "Massive context windows often leads to a distracted, confused, agent. This is context pollution. This is context overloading. This is toxic context.",
    "approx_timestamp": "4:51"
  },
  {
    "text": "Real engineering requires real developer workflows, not some pie in the sky God model that claims to do it all. And, you know, let me just spoil it for you. They won't. It's not here. It's not coming soon.",
    "approx_timestamp": "6:21"
  },
  {
    "text": "You give your agent the full 200K, 500K, 1 million tokens... you give your agent all of the space and the opportunity to solve the one problem well.",
    "approx_timestamp": "7:13"
  },
  {
    "text": "As agentic engineers, we have three constraints: the context window, the complexity of our codebase and the problem we're solving, and lastly our abilities. Specialized agents bypass two out of three of these.",
    "approx_timestamp": "7:34"
  },
  {
    "text": "Model intelligence is not a constraint. Don't use this as an excuse. It will set you back. This is a losing mindset.",
    "approx_timestamp": "7:50"
  },
  {
    "text": "The compact command inside of Claude Code... this is a bandaid fix. If your agent is running compact, it is losing information.",
    "approx_timestamp": "8:06"
  },
  {
    "text": "A focused engineer working on a single task is a productive engineer. Agents are the same... When your agent's performance drops, your performance drops. So let your agents focus.",
    "approx_timestamp": "8:30"
  },
  {
    "text": "You want to context engineer as little as possible. You want the minimum context in your prompt required to solve the problem.",
    "approx_timestamp": "8:54"
  },
  {
    "text": "You effectively create evals for the agentic layer of your codebase.",
    "approx_timestamp": "10:04"
  },
  {
    "text": "Everything comes down to the prompt level. All the ADW surrounding deterministic code is just support for the agents doing the real work.",
    "approx_timestamp": "20:16"
  },
  {
    "text": "These agents are very agreeable, but they're also just great at engineering... if you ask them to find something, they'll find something. You want to give them the tools to delineate what is important.",
    "approx_timestamp": "22:26"
  },
  {
    "text": "You don't want your agents bothering you. You want to reduce the number of human in the loop points there are in your workflow.",
    "approx_timestamp": "24:40"
  },
  {
    "text": "They're called out-loop systems for a reason. You want your agent shipping end to end. And if something goes wrong, you fix the end to end portion, not the thing that went wrong.",
    "approx_timestamp": "25:10"
  },
  {
    "text": "Why didn't you just do that by hand? You're missing the point... we are putting in the effort to build the system that builds the system.",
    "approx_timestamp": "37:30"
  },
  {
    "text": "This isn't about you anymore. This is about your agents. It's about teaching your agents how to build on your behalf, so that it can be about you again.",
    "approx_timestamp": "37:41"
  },
  {
    "text": "Our agents are fully connected, right, from planning to building to testing to reviewing to documenting their work. It's all connected now. This seals the deal. This connects them all.",
    "approx_timestamp": "47:39"
  },
  {
    "text": "Review answers the critical question: is what we built what we asked for? Even when your agent is wrong, when it presents you with proof that it thinks it's right, you can quickly say yes or no.",
    "approx_timestamp": "50:50"
  },
  {
    "text": "I'm not blaming the model for this. I'm blaming my skills. I'm blaming the ADWs we have set up right now.",
    "approx_timestamp": "50:30"
  },
  {
    "text": "What you want is a live asset that you can review that proves that what was built is what was asked for.",
    "approx_timestamp": "51:51"
  },
  {
    "text": "Ideally, you want two moments of presence: one at the beginning for your prompt input, one at the end for your review and your PR merge.",
    "approx_timestamp": "56:03"
  }
]
[
  {
    "q": "State the five SDLC steps as questions.",
    "a": "Plan: what are we building? Build: did we make it real? Test: does it work? Review: is what we built what we planned (prove it)? Document: how does it work?"
  },
  {
    "q": "What's the difference between testing and reviewing, and why does Dan refuse to group them?",
    "a": "Testing answers 'does it work?'; review answers 'is what we built what we asked for?' — spec conformance with proof, not functionality and not code quality. Grouping them is 'a massive mistake, especially in the age of agents' because they're different questions needing different specialist agents."
  },
  {
    "q": "What is Tactic #6, and what's the strongest single-sentence evidence it's already in the codebase?",
    "a": "One agent, one prompt, one purpose. 'No agent runs more than a single prompt' — every ADW phase spawns a named agent that executes exactly one slash-command template and exits."
  },
  {
    "q": "Name the three constraints of agentic engineers and which two the tactic bypasses.",
    "a": "The context window; the complexity of the codebase + problem; your abilities. Specialized agents bypass the context window (fresh, freed per step) and your abilities (engineering templated into committed prompts). Model intelligence is NOT a constraint — treating it as one is a losing mindset."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why does Dan call compact a band-aid?",
    "a": "If your agent is running compact, it is losing information — compression is lossy. One-purpose agents get fresh windows instead of compacted ones."
  },
  {
    "q": "What are the three review severity classes, and which ones block?",
    "a": "skippable, tech_debt, blocker. Only blockers set success=false, and only blockers get auto-resolved by the patch loop."
  },
  {
    "q": "Walk the review resolution loop, including its cap.",
    "a": "Run /review → parse JSON into ReviewResult → upload screenshots to R2 → post report. If blockers: for each blocker, create a patch plan (/patch) and implement it (/implement), commit, then re-review — up to MAX_REVIEW_RETRY_ATTEMPTS = 3 total attempts. If nothing resolves, stop; if blockers remain at the end, the workflow exits 1 and you fix the agentic layer for that class of issue."
  },
  {
    "q": "Why did the misplaced export button survive review, twice over?",
    "a": "First, the test step was skipped entirely (plan_build_review, not plan_build_test_review) — the UI test 'very likely would have caught this.' Second, the review agent captures screenshots it never reads: no --vision on Playwright MCP and no read-the-image instruction, so the tokens never flowed through the model's vision capabilities."
  },
  {
    "q": "How do you trigger a standalone patch out-loop, and what makes /patch different from /bug or /feature?",
    "a": "Comment (or write in the issue body) the keyword 'adw_patch' plus the change request, then run uv run adws/adw_patch.py <issue> [adw-id] (or let the webhook fire it). /patch creates a minimal, surgical plan in specs/patch/ scoped to only the review_change_request — a different class of change than chore/bug/feature — then reuses the same /implement higher-order prompt."
  },
  {
    "q": "What TWO things does the /document prompt produce or modify, and who consumes each?",
    "a": "It writes app_docs/feature-{adw_id}-{name}.md (with screenshots copied into app_docs/assets/) for humans AND future agents, and it appends an entry to .claude/commands/conditional_docs.md — which bug, chore, feature, patch, and prime read at planning time. That's the plan↔document feedback loop."
  },
  {
    "q": "What is the ADW state file, where does it live, and what fields does it carry?",
    "a": "agents/{adw_id}/adw_state.json — adw_id, issue_number, branch_name, plan_file, issue_class (review adds review_screenshots; document adds documentation_path). The ADW ID chains the state of all phases together."
  },
  {
    "q": "How does the codebase route models per prompt, and which prompts get the big model?",
    "a": "SLASH_COMMAND_MODEL_MAP in adw_modules/agent.py: opus for /implement, /review, /bug, /feature, /patch; sonnet for /chore, /document, tests, classification and git ops; default sonnet. Per-purpose agents make per-step model routing (and eval-style reruns) trivial."
  },
  {
    "q": "What's the ideal presence count and what are the two moments?",
    "a": "Two: one at the beginning for prompt input, one at the end for review and PR merge."
  },
  {
    "q": "What proof does review owe you, and what does that do to your KPIs?",
    "a": "A hard/live asset proving the work matches the ask — 1-5 screenshots here; a report, video, or metrics for non-UI domains. Proof lets you say yes/no in seconds even when the agent is wrong, which directly increases review velocity (and drives presence down)."
  }
]
